# Line endings, pinned at the repository rather than per machine.
#
# Without this file the answer is `core.autocrlf`, which is a setting on whoever
# happens to be cloning. On Windows it defaults to `true`, and this repository
# was developed with it on: every blob in git is LF -- 289 of 289 text files,
# checked rather than assumed -- while a Windows working tree held 236 files as
# CRLF and 52 as LF, the split being simply which files a tool had rewritten
# since the last checkout. Nothing was wrong in git and nothing was consistent
# on disk.
#
# **It was not only cosmetic, and the exception is the reason this file names
# `*.pdf` explicitly.** `src-tauri/src/warm.pdf` is a tracked PDF that
# `worker_child.rs` pulls in with `include_bytes!`, so it ships inside the
# binary. Git classifies a file as text when its first 8000 bytes hold no NUL,
# and a small uncompressed PDF holds none -- so `autocrlf` converted it on
# checkout, and every Windows clone compiled a *different* file into the
# executable than the one in git:
#
#     blob          571 bytes   qpdf --check: no syntax or stream encoding errors
#     working tree  603 bytes   qpdf --check: file is damaged, xref not found,
#                               attempting to reconstruct cross-reference table
#
# The 32 inserted bytes move every object past them, so the offsets in the xref
# table no longer address what they name. PDFium reconstructs at least as
# willingly as qpdf did, and `warm_fonts` returns silently when a load fails, so
# this had no symptom anybody could see -- which is what let it last.
#
# The default: git decides what is text, and every text file checks out LF on
# every platform. Both halves matter. `eol=lf` alone would still let git guess
# wrongly about a file like the one above; `binary` below is what stops the
# guess being made at all where we already know the answer.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Bytes we know are bytes. `binary` is `-diff -merge -text`: no conversion, and
# no attempt to show a diff that would be noise. Listed by extension rather than
# by path so a second icon or fixture is covered the day it arrives, and the
# extensions here are the tracked ones plus the near neighbours somebody will
# add without thinking about this file.
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.ico   binary
*.icns  binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.dll   binary
*.dylib binary
*.so    binary

# Shell scripts get their own line for a reason that is not tidiness: a CRLF
# `.sh` is not merely different, it is unrunnable under WSL bash, which fails at
# the first line with `$'\r': command not found` while Git Bash tolerates it --
# so the same file works in one shell and is broken in another. None is tracked
# today; this is here so the first one to arrive is right.
*.sh    text eol=lf
